 right but it's still dominated by  one uh one fruit so it's low evenness  but high richness and this last example  is where it's both  rich and evenness so this is an example  where  uh  this high rich and high evenness would  have the highest greatest diversity  score alpha diversity score uh compared  to  high richness and low evenness so both  of these matter at least for some  indices of alpha diversity  so  as we said you know what this is saying  is from the first trimester to the third  trimester the alpha diversity or the  richness is decreased  how about beta diversity so they also  looked at uh beta diversity and the next  slide we'll talk about what that is but  it's really there is the diversity  between mothers right so when we're  talking about alpha diversity it is  really within a sample  within a specific sample here we're  comparing two different samples so the  diversity between mothers increased from  trimester one to time trimester three so  let's look at  the concept of beta diversity very  briefly again  it is a measure of similarity between  the communities of two different samples  so how many organisms so this is a  property of two different things so how  many organisms are shared between the  two samples  and then whether the organisms shared  are present in in the same abundance so  it also has this concept of  same abundance so the higher the beta  diversity the less similar the  communities are  and then beta diversity is high in the  gut so different people have different  microbes because there's a lot of  choices to choose from as we said  earlier because so they're less similar  to each other  compared to  beta diversity in a different or  different body site for example  uh so what they saw here was there was a  vast expansion of diversity between  mothers so from trimester one  uh the trimester one mothers were more  similar to each other compared to the  trimester 3m  microbiota  and then there was an overall increase  in proteobacteria and actinobacteria in  trimester 3.  so really what these findings indicate  is that by trimester three the  microbiotas were depleted of bacterial  phylogenetic diversity this is alpha  diversity is depleted but in ways that  differ between women so that that's the  increase in  beta diversity that the second part is  referring to  uh so the study they also looked at  there were 29 different um  otus or species whose relative abundance  reliably discriminated between trimester  one and trimester three and if you're  wondering whether trimester one or  trimester three is more similar to the  non-pregnant uh  non-pregnant woman it was trimester one  so trimester one was more similar to the  non-pregnant woman  and then they also did a  transplant a fecal transplant in  germ-free mice  where they transplanted either trimester  3 from the moms these are human pregnant  moms either the trimester three fecal  samples or trimester one fecal samples  and then they looked at inflammation and  adiposity and what they saw was when you  transferred trimester three microbiota  that that increased that resulted in  greater inflammation and increased  adiposity compared to when when you  transplanted the t1 uh the trimester one  microbiota so  the conclusion of the authors or  gestational age related changes in the  microbiota that promote weight gain  suggest that you know  as as pregnancy is a unique state  um they require greater energy harvest  so the microbiota  have been  adapted to help with the greater energy  harvest needed during pregnancy to  support the growth of the mother and the  fetus so this is again uh micro  uh the composition uh related to the  function and that that we're getting at  here  there was another study that was  published in 2015  this was a case control study of 49  pregnant women and in this study instead  of just you know last study just got one  sample on trimester one and one sample  in trimester three here they instead got  weekly samples on during pregnancy and  it was not just stool samples they also  got  these additional samples that we won't  talk about today  but they had weekly samples and then  they looked at alpha diversity they  looked at beta diversity and they also  looked at  what they called instability so this is  really  also a way of looking at beta diversity  but it's looking at how the samples  differ  between the same subject sample  over time so you're looking at same  subject community sampled in consecutive  weeks  so here's the result from that study  and then you know  i won't talk about these other other  body sites other than to say you know  the message was largely similar but what  you can see is if you look at alpha  diversity beta diversity or  or instability or beta diversity  for the most part they seem pretty  stable so in the x-axis is gestational  weeks of pregnancy  so you can see that for the most part  they seem pretty stable so the stability  and the beta diversity especially you  know the p-values also support that and  then for the alpha diversity there could  be a slight decrease uh you know again  the p-value is it's not significant  but it is 0.053  so there could be a trend for decreased  alpha diversity  but the overall message is this looks  you know if you look at the stability  over week that it looks pretty stable  during this time period so how do you  bring these two different studies that  we just talked about together  and one way to think about this is that  trimester one versus trimester three  which is that first study uh there are  likely differences in the gut microbiota  and microbiome and this makes sense  there's  major differences in physiological needs  of the mom and the fetus between  trimester one which is which is uh much  more similar to the non-pregnant stage  compared to trimester three  uh but if you think about the  differences between trimester two and  trimester three there might not be as  many differences  not just t