g to  look at the role of again some of the  same markers so impaired gut integrity  and inflammation  in this study we specifically looked at  again marker of  gut barrier dysfunction ifab  and then two markers of  monocyte activation  and what you see is that and this is in  a population with hiv and what we see  here is that uh higher levels of again  markers of monocyte activation and  intestinal barrier uh dysfunction are  associated with increased preterm birth  so if you have higher levels of these  markers during pregnancy  uh you're more likely to deliver a baby  that is pre-term birth  so it sounds like you know  gut dysfunction and inflammation also  plays a role in pregnancy outcomes  in hiv in populations with hiv  so how about more specifically related  to gut dysbiosis so given what we just  talked about the impact of  hiv on blood function  a related question then is is there a  difference in the microbiota profile of  pregnant woman by hiv status  because there are differences among  adults so this would be you know  extending that to a population with  pregnancy  which is a special uh stage of life uh  there there's uh different you know  physiological changes happening hormonal  changes that are happening so  it's not a guarantee that whatever is  happening in adults is also happening  during pregnancy  and then if there is uh differences in  the microbiota profile by hiv status  could these differences be contributing  to some of the adverse outcomes that you  see in maternal infant populations with  hiv  so for example as i said earlier hiv  infected  pregnant women they deliver more preterm  birth babies than uninfected women  they also have babies that are lower uh  that have that have a low birth weight  compared to women without hiv  in addition after the infants are born  uh the infants actually also have growth  deficits  for example there's more stunting uh  there is more wasting uh  underweight so uh  the infants although the infants do 